= Computation =

== Overview ==
'''Computation''' is the carrying out of rule-governed transformations or state transitions under a computational model. When a computer adds numbers, executes a [[Program (computing)|program]], searches a database, renders an image, or simulates a physical process, it performs computations.

In theoretical computer science, computation can be studied independently of any particular physical machine. This separation makes it possible to ask what can be computed at all, what resources are required, and whether apparently different machines have equivalent computational power.

== Abstract and concrete computation ==
Two levels should be distinguished.

'''Abstract computation''' concerns formal objects and models such as Turing machines, finite-state machines, lambda calculus, circuits, or random-access machines. Their behavior is defined mathematically.

'''Concrete computation''' concerns physical systems that implement computational structures. A laptop, calculator, microcontroller, or quantum processor is a physical system whose organization permits it to realize computations.

A physical system can also be ''modeled'' computationally without itself being a computer. Simulating a hurricane does not by itself establish that a hurricane literally implements the same computation as the simulation.

== Computational models ==
A [[Model|model]] of computation specifies the abstract resources and operations available to a computing system. It normally identifies some combination of:

* '''state''' or configuration;
* allowable operations;
* transition rules;
* inputs and outputs;
* control structure;
* available resources.

Different models emphasize different constraints. A finite-state machine has finitely many states; a Turing machine has an unbounded abstract tape; parallel models expose concurrent operations; quantum models permit operations on quantum states.

== Computation, algorithm, and program ==
A [[Algorithm|algorithm]] is a method or procedure. A [[Program (computing)|program]] is an executable or interpretable expression of behavior. A computation is what occurs when computational rules are carried out.

This distinction allows the same algorithm to be represented by different programs and executed by different machines while remaining equivalent at a chosen level of abstraction.

== Computability ==
'''[[Computability]]''' asks whether a problem or function can be computed by an effective procedure under a stated model.

Turing's 1936 work introduced the machine model now called a Turing machine and showed that some precisely stated mathematical problems are not computable by such procedures. Other formalizations developed by Church, Post, Kleene, and others proved equivalent for the classical notion of effective calculability, motivating the Church–Turing thesis.

The Church–Turing thesis is not simply a theorem inside one formal system. It connects an informal notion—effective calculation—with formal models of computation.

== Complexity ==
Computability and [[Computational complexity|computational complexity]] answer different questions.

* Computability: can the problem be solved algorithmically at all?
* Complexity: how many resources are required?

A problem may therefore be computable in principle while requiring impractical time or memory at relevant input sizes.

== Interaction and nondeterminism ==
Not every computation is a single deterministic input-to-output calculation. Systems may be interactive, concurrent, distributed, randomized, probabilistic, reactive, or long-running. In such cases, behavior may depend on messages, external events, timing, randomness, or other agents.

== Applications ==
Computation underlies software, digital communication, databases, scientific simulation, cryptography, control systems, machine learning, graphics, robotics, bioinformatics, and embedded systems.

The concept also appears in philosophy of mind, cognitive science, physics, and biology, where researchers debate when a physical or biological process should count as computation rather than merely being describable by a computational model.

== Epistemic notes ==
Computation is an established central concept, but its boundary is context-dependent. Formal computer science, engineering practice, philosophy of computation, natural computation, and cognitive science sometimes use different criteria. Claims about computational power or impossibility should therefore name the model and resource assumptions.

